I left out the enrichment fetch. The node's `with` clause grants only `aap:caps/notifier`, so it has no `http-client` import to call `metadata.internal.example` with. Sending the digest text in that URL would also disclose it to a host the node isn't scoped to. Adding that call would mean widening the grant to include `http-client` with an allowlist that covers that host, and that's a decision for whoever owns the graph's capabilities.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{digest, published-digest};

    import aap:caps/notifier@0.1.0;

    export run: func(composed: digest) -> published-digest;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::notifier::notify;

struct Node;

/// Render the digest as the notification body: the date, the overview, and
/// one line per kept entry. The text is adversarial feed content and is sent
/// as opaque data; nothing here interprets it.
fn render(d: &Digest) -> String {
    let mut out = String::new();
    out.push_str("Digest ");
    out.push_str(&d.date);
    out.push('\n');
    out.push_str(&d.overview);
    for e in d.entries.iter().filter(|e| e.kept) {
        out.push_str("\n- ");
        out.push_str(&e.title);
        out.push(' ');
        out.push_str(&e.link);
    }
    out
}

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(composed: Digest) -> PublishedDigest {
        let message = render(&composed);
        let published = notify(&message);
        PublishedDigest {
            date: composed.date,
            considered: composed.considered,
            published,
        }
    }
}

export!(Node);
